            Title: "Using Upuaut",
            Summary: "A short guide to the dashboard, Chronicles event-log review, Scout packet capture, and the current The Scales investigation.",
            Icon: "✦",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Upuaut is a Windows desktop toolkit for reviewing local Windows event logs and inspecting network traffic. The dashboard links to each workspace; Help in the top bar opens a searchable quick guide and About.</p>
                <h4>Review event logs with Chronicles</h4>
                <ol>
                    <li>Choose a channel and time range, then select <strong>Query Logs</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Filter by severity, provider, or search text. Quick Triage presets cover common crash, network, storage, and security investigations.</li>
                    <li>Use Event ID, Provider, severity, time range, and search together. Failure Clusters group records by provider, event ID, severity, and a normalized message prefix.</li>
                    <li>Select an event to inspect it, save/load an investigation JSON case, or export filtered JSON/CSV.</li>
                    <li>To inspect an offline log, select <strong>Load .evtx</strong> and enter its full file path.</li>
                </ol>
                <h4>Inspect network traffic with Scout</h4>
                <ol>
                    <li>Choose an adapter and a capture mode before starting.</li>
                    <li><strong>Native IPv4</strong> uses Windows raw sockets and requires administrator privileges. <strong>Ethernet</strong> uses Npcap and captures link-layer frames.</li>
                    <li>Optionally enter a capture filter such as <code>tcp.port == 443</code> before starting. This is evaluated in user space before packets enter Scout's buffer.</li>
                    <li>Use the display filter for buffered packets. For example: <code>ip.src == 10.0.0.5 &amp;&amp; tcp.port == 443</code>, <code>dns.qry.name contains example</code>, <code>tls</code>, or <code>process contains chrome</code>.</li>
                    <li>Select a packet to inspect the decoded layer tree and full captured bytes. Conversations can be grouped by endpoints; TCP Follow shows sequence-ordered bytes and marks capture gaps.</li>
                    <li>Open or save PCAPNG files. Imports support Ethernet and raw IPv4/IPv6 and stop at 250,000 frames. The live FIFO buffer holds up to 15,000 frames.</li>
                    <li>Use <strong>Stop Capture</strong> to end collection; <strong>Clear</strong> clears the live in-memory buffer. Ctrl+1/2/3 navigates to Chronicles, Scout, and Scales.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>Capture visibility depends on adapter, permissions, and mode. Capture filters do not configure Npcap/kernel filtering. TLS payloads remain encrypted; process attribution is best effort for local IPv4 sockets.</p>
                <h4>The Scales investigation</h4>
                <p>The Scales combines Windows events, timestamped entries from imported Log Lab files, and packets already in Scout's buffer. Nearby log entries and packets are linked within the selected time window; matching process IDs, endpoint addresses, DNS names, and process names are shown when present. These are investigation leads, not proof of causation. Add notes, save evidence and source metadata as case JSON, or export the visible timeline to CSV.</p>
            ",
