        new(
            Id: "general-purpose-log-lab",
            Category: "log-lab",
            CategoryName: "General-purpose Log Lab",
            Title: "Import and inspect logs in Log Lab",
            Summary: "Import unfamiliar logs, inspect extracted fields, review scenario labels, and keep the original source available when parsing is incomplete.",
            Icon: "⌕",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Open <strong>Log Lab</strong> from the navigation and import a log file. Log Lab recognizes plain text, JSON, JSON Lines (NDJSON), XML including repeated Windows Event XML records, CSV, INI sections, logfmt key/value records, RFC 3164/5424 syslog, and Apache combined access logs. Files are limited to 32 MiB, and parsing stops after 200,000 entries. The displayed file type comes from its extension; unknown extensions remain identified while the content is inspected as plain text. User parser profiles can handle additional line-oriented formats.</p>
                <p>Unknown formats and malformed structured logs fall back to decoded raw text. Readable non-log files such as solution files, hex dumps, and mostly textual save files remain available in the editor. Upuaut checks encoding and readable-text proportion: predominantly binary or encrypted files skip structured parsing and show a warning with a decoded raw preview. UTF-16/32 BOM-less text is detected where its byte pattern is clear. If scenario or timeline analysis fails, the original source stays open in the raw editor and the reason is recorded in Upuaut diagnostics.</p>
                <h4>Search and inspect</h4>
                <ol>
                    <li>Search across entry messages, raw lines, and extracted fields. Use the severity selector to narrow the list.</li>
                    <li>Select an entry to see its source line or line range, normalized fields, and a short explanation of what the parser recognized.</li>
                    <li>Choose <strong>Full Source</strong> to review the complete imported text. Select an entry again or choose <strong>Selected Entry</strong> to return to its raw content.</li>
                </ol>
                <h4>Format without changing the import</h4>
                <p>The editor is a working view. Syntax coloring marks common timestamps, severity words, field names, codes, strings, and structural delimiters; it is best-effort and automatically pauses on sources over 300,000 characters. Format JSON or XML, search forward or backward, replace one or all matches, copy, or save an edited copy. Use <strong>Reset Editor</strong> to restore the selected entry or original source. Formatting and edits never change the imported source used by the parser. Toggle wrapping independently for the raw editor and entry results.</p>
                <h4>Review scenario analysis</h4>
                <p>Each entry receives a best-effort scenario label where supported, such as crash, setup, update, authentication, security, network, storage, service, performance, maintenance, or success. The analysis shows the phrase signals and source fields that matched, tags, and suggested checks. Filter entries by scenario using the summary chips or selector.</p>
                <p><strong>Match strength</strong> indicates how many rule signals matched the entry. It is not confidence in a root cause, proof of a security incident, or confirmation that a reported operation succeeded. Compare the finding with the raw source and surrounding entries.</p>
                <h4>Review event sequences and save reports</h4>
                <p>Sequence Review groups repeated error signatures and highlights nearby warning→error or error→success entries in source order. Select a finding to jump to its first source entry. These patterns help organize review; they do not establish causation. Save a Markdown summary or JSON report from the report buttons. Reports include log text and evidence, so inspect them before sharing. Markdown lists up to 200 notable entries; JSON includes up to 10,000 notable entries, with the omitted count recorded in the report.</p>
                <h4>Create a custom parser profile</h4>
                <p>Expand <strong>User Parser Profiles</strong> and choose a built-in template or edit the JSON example. Built-ins cover .NET console, Apache error, Nginx access errors, and RFC 3164 syslog formats. A profile has a name, a <code>linePattern</code>, and field extraction rules. The example uses named regex capture groups called <code>value</code>; set <code>groupName</code> to another named group when needed. Save profiles locally to this Windows account, load a profile into the editor, or apply the current JSON to the original imported source. Applying a profile creates a separate derived list and leaves the raw source intact.</p>
                <p>Patterns are case-insensitive, limited to 512 characters, and run with a 50 ms per-match timeout and an 8-second total application budget. A profile can define up to 32 field rules; the account can store up to 30 profiles. Expensive patterns may time out or stop before all lines are processed.</p>
                <h4>Compare sequences and tune reports</h4>
                <p>Select a sequence finding to compare its first two linked entries side by side, including source lines, scenario labels, and matched evidence. Open either event in the inspector for its full parsed fields. The report content options control summary counts, category totals, sequence findings, notable entries, suggested checks, and parser notes. Markdown reports include up to 200 notable entries; JSON reports include up to 10,000 and record the number omitted.</p>
                <h4>Navigate the timeline and save filters</h4>
                <p>The UTC timeline groups timestamped entries into bounded buckets. Choose an interval or click a bucket to add its time range to the active filters. Advanced filters combine text, severity, scenario, component/source, and local-time boundaries. Save the current combination with a name, then load it later from the saved-filter list; up to 50 are stored for this Windows account.</p>
                <p>Sequence Review lets you set a timestamp window and optionally require both entries to share the same parsed component/source. When timestamps are absent, the analyzer uses a limited 40-source-line adjacency fallback; the selected time window cannot be inferred for those entries. Reports record the active sequence settings and can include the timeline trend table.</p>
                <h4>Interpretation limits</h4>
                <p>Extraction, classification, and sequence grouping are best-effort, and format detection can be uncertain. Review parser notes and compare fields with the raw source before drawing conclusions.</p>
            ",
            RawText: "Log Lab general purpose log parsing robust raw fallback unknown format malformed binary import text plain text JSON JSONL NDJSON XML CSV source line provenance severity search fields raw editor full source pretty format JSON XML reset 32 MiB 200000 entries best effort parser notes scenario classification evidence tags recommendations sequence repeated failures warning error recovery reports Markdown JSON custom regex parser profiles field extraction timeout built-in .NET console Apache error Nginx access RFC 3164 syslog side by side report sections timeline UTC time buckets component filter source filter time range saved search filter presets sequence window same component"
