            Id: "crash-forensics",
            Category: "crash-forensics",
            CategoryName: "Crash Forensics",
            Title: "Inspect Windows crash artifacts",
            Summary: "Read Windows user-mode minidumps and WER reports with bounded local parsing and source-backed interpretation.",
            Icon: "ð“ƒ£",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Open <strong>Crash Forensics</strong> and choose a Windows <code>.dmp</code>, <code>.mdmp</code>, or <code>.wer</code> report. Upuaut checks file signatures before parsing, reads the source without modifying it, and keeps dump content on this computer.</p>
                <h4>Review a minidump</h4>
                <p>The reader extracts available architecture, Windows version, timestamp, process ID, exception code and parameters, module metadata, and thread metadata. If a managed CLR runtime is present, Upuaut attempts bounded managed thread and stack inspection. Dumps often omit memory, runtime, or symbol data; missing evidence is reported as a warning rather than inferred.</p>
                <p>Managed symbol lookup is optional and off by default. Enabling it permits requests to Microsoft's HTTPS symbol server and stores symbols in the current user's local Upuaut cache. The dump itself is not uploaded. Kernel dump signatures are identified, but reliable kernel/driver analysis is handed off to WinDbg.</p>
                <h4>Review a WER report and link log evidence</h4>
                <p>Windows Error Reporting <code>.wer</code> files reuse Log Lab's parser and versioned knowledge set. Extracted fields, source lines, and matching explanations are displayed alongside recognized exception codes. In Log Lab, choose a crash-specific entry and use <strong>Open this finding in Crash Forensics</strong> to carry a concise copy as contextual evidence. That relationship is a review aid, not proof that the entry caused the crash.</p>
                <p>Export a Markdown report after reviewing it for usernames, local paths, and other sensitive information. Input sizes are capped, metadata lists and stack frames are bounded, and corrupt or unsupported artifacts produce a clear error while leaving the original file untouched.</p>
            ",
