        new(
            Id: "the-duat-chronicles",
            Category: "chronicles",
            CategoryName: "Duat Chronicles",
            Title: "The Duat Chronicles & Forensic Event Log Engine",
            Summary: "Streaming Windows Event Log parser using native EventLogReader, XPath optimization, and deep forensic error explanation.",
            Icon: "📜",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>The standard Windows Event Viewer (<code>eventvwr.msc</code>) is notorious for slow, blocking scans, cryptic error messages, and an overwhelming flood of noise. The Duat Chronicles revolutionizes Windows log analysis through structured, high-speed parsing.</p>
                
                <h4>1. Optimized XPath Queries</h4>
                <p>Instead of reading logs sequentially, Upuaut uses compiled XPath expressions evaluated directly by the Windows Eventing kernel subsystem (<code>wevtapi.dll</code>):</p>
                <pre class=""docs-code""><code>*[System[(Level=1 or Level=2) and TimeCreated[timediff(@SystemTime) &lt;= 86400000]]]</code></pre>
                <p>The service applies supported channel, severity, time, provider, and event ID filters before returning records. Query time depends on the selected channel, event volume, machine, and Windows Event Log service.</p>

                <h4>2. Quick-Triage Presets</h4>
                <p>One-click filters isolate the most urgent OS failures:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>⚡ BSOD &amp; BugChecks:</strong> Extracts Event 1001 (WER-SystemErrorReporting) and Event 41 (Kernel-Power).</li>
                    <li><strong>💥 App Crashes:</strong> Isolates Event 1000 (Application Error) and Event 1026 (.NET Runtime).</li>
                    <li><strong>🌐 Network &amp; DNS:</strong> Catches Event 1014 (DNS Client Resolution Timeout) and NetBT failures.</li>
                    <li><strong>💾 Disk &amp; Storage:</strong> Identifies NTFS corruption, bad blocks (Event 7 / Disk), and StorAHCI timeouts.</li>
                    <li><strong>🔐 Security:</strong> Tracks Event 4625 (Failed Logons) with Logon Type 2/3/10 and Sub-Status codes.</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>3. Evidence-based explanations</h4>
                <p>Where a known event pattern matches, Chronicles provides a readable category and headline, the fields used as evidence, possible explanations, and investigation steps. Cause descriptions are hypotheses; a matched rule is not a probability. Commands are shown only when the event provides enough context to make a specific diagnostic command appropriate.</p>

                <h4>4. Structured queries, presets &amp; cases</h4>
                <p>Use Event ID, provider, severity, channel, time range (including a custom start/end), and free text together. Save a named filter preset in this Windows user profile for repeated queries. Save an investigation JSON case to preserve the filter and displayed event records; load it later to continue offline review. Filtered records can be exported as JSON or CSV.</p>
                <p>Failure Clusters are grouped by provider, event ID, severity, and the first 80 characters of a message after replacing numbers and GUIDs. A repeated signature is a useful grouping clue, not proof of a common cause.</p>
            ",
            RawText: "The Duat Chronicles EventLogReader XPath optimization structured Event ID provider custom time named filter presets investigation JSON case export CSV failure clusters grouping signature"
        ),

        new(
            Id: "the-scout-packet-capture",
            Category: "scout",
            CategoryName: "The Scout",
            Title: "The Scout: Driverless Packet Capture & Process Attribution",
            Summary: "Native IPv4 raw socket capture plus optional Npcap Ethernet capture, protocol decoding, packet inspection, and best-effort process attribution.",
            Icon: "📡",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Scout provides two capture modes. Native IPv4 uses Windows raw sockets without an added capture driver. Ethernet mode uses Npcap to capture link-layer frames.</p>

                <h4>1. Driverless Capture via SIO_RCVALL</h4>
                <p>When running with administrative privileges, Upuaut binds a standard .NET <code>System.Net.Sockets.Socket</code> configured as:</p>
                <pre class=""docs-code""><code>var socket = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Raw, ProtocolType.IP);
socket.Bind(new IPEndPoint(localIp, 0));
socket.IOControl(IOControlCode.ReceiveAll, new byte[] { 1, 0, 0, 0 }, new byte[] { 1, 0, 0, 0 });</code></pre>
                <p>This asks the Windows TCP/IP stack to deliver IPv4 traffic visible to the selected interface. Raw-socket access requires elevated permissions; adapter and Windows filtering affect which traffic is visible.</p>

                <h4>2. Optional Ethernet Capture</h4>
                <p>Select <strong>Ethernet frames (Npcap)</strong> when Npcap is installed and its capture service is available. It exposes Ethernet/VLAN details and dissects IPv4, IPv6 extension headers, ARP, TCP, UDP, and ICMPv6. Raw socket mode remains IPv4-only.</p>

                <h4>3. Process Attribution (iphlpapi.dll)</h4>
                <p>Scout consults the Windows TCP and UDP endpoint tables to associate packets with local processes where a current socket matches the packet endpoints. Attribution may be unavailable for unmatched or closed sockets. Resolved details can include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Process ID (PID) and Process Name (e.g. <code>chrome.exe</code>, <code>teams.exe</code>, <code>svchost.exe</code>).</li>
                    <li>Full executable image path on disk.</li>
                    <li>Active TCP state (Established, TimeWait, CloseWait, Listen).</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>4. Filters, conversations &amp; packet inspection</h4>
                <p>Capture filters are user-space predicates applied before buffering; they do not reduce packets delivered by the capture backend. Display filters support common fields joined with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>: <code>ip.src == 10.0.0.5</code>, <code>tcp.port == 443</code>, <code>udp.dstport == 53</code>, <code>dns.qry.name contains example</code>, <code>tls</code>, and <code>process contains chrome</code>. Unsupported syntax is reported inline.</p>
                <p>The bounded live ring buffer retains up to 15,000 frames and reports FIFO overwrites, user-space filtered frames, parser rejects, and socket errors. Conversations group packets by endpoint pair. TCP Follow orders segments by sequence number, trims overlaps, and marks sequence gaps; it cannot restore packets that were never captured. The inspector decodes bounded DNS question/answer fields, selected HTTP metadata, and TLS ClientHello server names when those fields are present in a captured segment. Query strings and credential headers are omitted from HTTP evidence. TLS application data remains encrypted, and these decoders do not reassemble split application messages.</p>
                <p>Open and save PCAPNG captures using Ethernet or raw IP link types. Imports accept IPv4/IPv6 raw packets and Ethernet, and are limited to 250,000 frames. The packet inspector shows decoded fields and full frame bytes.</p>
            ",
            RawText: "The Scout Packet Capture SIO_RCVALL raw sockets driverless Ethernet Npcap IPv4 IPv6 ARP ICMPv6 TCP UDP DNS TLS filters capture display PCAPNG pcap follow stream conversation ring buffer IP Helper best effort PID attribution"
        ),

        new(
            Id: "diagnostics-and-crash-reporting",
            Category: "diagnostics",
            CategoryName: "Diagnostics & Telemetry",
            Title: "App Diagnostics & Forensic Crash Reporting",
            Summary: "Comprehensive telemetry system with in-memory breadcrumbs, rolling JSONL logs, and multi-threaded crash trapping.",
            Icon: "🛡️",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Upuaut is designed with industrial-grade resilience. Diagnostic and forensic crash data are recorded continuously so that any failure can be analyzed post-mortem.</p>

                <h4>1. Multi-Threaded Crash Hooks</h4>
                <p>Upuaut records crash details and recent diagnostic breadcrumbs when an exception reaches its configured crash handlers:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>WpfApp.DispatcherUnhandledException</code>: Traps UI thread faults without tearing down the native window.</li>
                    <li><code>AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException</code>: Catches worker thread and background task exceptions.</li>
                    <li><code>TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException</code>: Intercepts unobserved asynchronous faults.</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>2. Forensic Dump Vault</h4>
                <p>When an unhandled exception occurs, Upuaut writes a comprehensive forensic dump under <code>%LocalAppData%\Upuaut\Crashes</code> containing:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Machine &amp; OS Telemetry:</strong> Windows Build, .NET runtime version, CPU architecture, elevation status, and physical RAM footprint.</li>
                    <li><strong>Exception Stack Trace:</strong> Full inner exception chain with source file line numbers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recent Breadcrumbs:</strong> Up to the last 50 in-memory diagnostic events available when the report is created.</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>3. Daily Rolling Logs</h4>
                <p>Telemetry is written to <code>%LocalAppData%\Upuaut\Logs\upuaut-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl</code>. Each entry is a single-line JSON object containing timestamps, thread IDs, log levels, calling methods, and telemetry payloads.</p>
            ",
            RawText: "Crash Reporter Diagnostics Telemetry LocalAppData Upuaut Crashes Logs JSONL Breadcrumbs Dispatcher AppDomain TaskScheduler"
        ),

        new(
            Id: "visual-studio-2026-guide",
            Category: "vs2026",
            CategoryName: "Visual Studio 2026",
            Title: "Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise Edition Developer Guide",
            Summary: "Configuration guide for Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise Edition, including .slnx solution structure, .editorconfig, and launch profiles.",
            Icon: "💻",
            HtmlContent: @"
                <p>Upuaut is configured for development in <strong>Visual Studio 2026 Enterprise Edition</strong> (as well as VS 2022 v17.10+):</p>

                <h4>1. Modern Solution Structure (.slnx)</h4>
                <p>The solution file <code>Upuaut.slnx</code> uses the new streamlined XML solution format. Projects are logically partitioned into <code>/src/</code> and <code>/tests/</code> folders:</p>
                <ul>
